Archaeologists find recycling surprisingly difficult to measure. A workshop floor containing offcuts, drips and failed vessels demonstrates local glassworking, but those remains do not reveal what proportion of the batch was old glass. Conversely, efficient recycling may leave little discarded cullet to excavate. Chemical analysis supplies indirect clues. Roman colourless glass was commonly decolourised with either antimony or manganese. When fragments contain signatures associated with both additives, researchers may infer that two formerly separate glass groups were mixed during remelting. Elevated traces of colouring metals can likewise suggest that coloured scraps entered an otherwise pale batch. Yet such signals are not automatic proof: glass of similar composition can be recycled together without producing an obvious mixed signature.

Other evidence makes the practice more visible. The second-century wreck Iulia Felix carried cullet separated by colour and type, indicating that broken glass itself was traded. At sites such as Roman Tomis, glassworking waste and compositions consistent with mixing point to secondary production and recycling. The combined evidence therefore supports a recycling economy, while also warning against treating every chemically uniform vessel as newly made. Recycling can alter a composition, but it can also conceal itself by repeatedly mixing like with like.', 0);

The work was divided historically, but descriptions differ by place and period. One museum account records men collecting bark while women wove, sewed and decorated clothing; other records describe women collecting bark as well. Such variation cautions against turning one documented practice into a universal rule. Production was laborious, and a single garment could require months of work. The resulting cloth was valued for durability, water resistance and breathability. It supplied everyday and ceremonial clothing and, in the Edo and Meiji periods, also became a traded material used beyond Ainu communities.

Decoration could transform the plain brown cloth. Dark cotton appliqué and embroidery were added around areas such as the collar, cuffs and back, with patterns varying regionally and being transmitted between generations. Cotton itself came through exchange and did not replace bark fibre in every garment. Today, makers continue attus production while adapting the cloth to objects such as sashes, wallets and coasters. This continuity should not be mistaken for mechanical uniformity: research at the National Ainu Museum stresses that techniques vary with individual teachers, regions and makers.', 1);

Earlier Ladakhi schemes associated with engineer Chewang Norphel spread water across shaded, high-altitude slopes to freeze in horizontal layers. Sonam Wangchuk''s later ice-stupa design kept the principle of winter storage but changed the geometry and location. Water from a source above a village travels downhill through a pipe. The height difference creates pressure, so at the lower end water can spray upward without an electric pump. In sufficiently cold air, falling droplets freeze around a simple framework, gradually building a roughly conical tower. Its resemblance to a Buddhist stupa gave the structure its name.

The cone is not immune to melting; melting is its intended method of delivery. Its advantage is that a compact, steep-sided volume exposes less surface area to sun and warm air than a shallow sheet holding the same amount of ice. It can therefore persist farther into spring. Actual performance still depends on weather, water supply and construction. Energy-balance research on artificial ice reservoirs shows that wind, humidity, temperature and radiation affect both growth and decay, so shape alone cannot guarantee a particular release date. Ice stupas bridge a seasonal gap; they do not manufacture water, nor can they work after the feeder stream itself disappears.', 2);

Project Habbakuk proposed a refrigerated vessel on a scale closer to a floating island than a conventional carrier. A prototype was built on Patricia Lake in Alberta, but it was not a miniature warship; it was a block-like test structure used to study construction and preservation. The attractive phrase ''ship made of ice'' also concealed complications. A full vessel would still need insulation, refrigeration pipes, structural materials and machinery. Damage might be repaired by refreezing a water-pulp mixture, yet the same need to keep the hull frozen imposed a continuous engineering burden.

The project ended before a full-sized carrier was built. Its cancellation did not show that pykrete lacked unusual properties. Rather, the strategic problem and the available alternatives changed while estimates of cost and complexity rose. Longer-range aircraft, escort carriers, improved radar and additional bases helped close the protection gap that had justified the scheme. Habbakuk therefore illustrates a distinction between material feasibility and system usefulness: a substance can perform impressively in a test while the enormous machine designed around it becomes unnecessary or uneconomical.', 3);

Several conditions can preserve this separation. Salty deep water may be denser than fresher surface water; groundwater can introduce dissolved minerals at depth; or a deep, sheltered basin may resist wind-driven circulation. Temperature can still change within such a lake, but seasonal cooling is insufficient to overcome the stabilising density difference. Meromixis therefore describes incomplete mixing, not a lake that is motionless everywhere. The upper layer may circulate vigorously while the deepest water remains cut off.

Isolation has chemical and biological consequences. Organic matter falling from the productive surface decomposes below. Because oxygen from the atmosphere is not regularly carried to the bottom, decomposition can consume the remaining supply, leaving the monimolimnion anoxic. Reduced compounds and nutrients may accumulate, while specialised microorganisms occupy the sharp gradients near the chemocline. Fine sediment layers can also remain less disturbed than in a frequently mixed lake. These features make meromictic lakes useful natural laboratories for studying microbial metabolism and past environmental conditions. However, ''persistent'' does not mean literally eternal: unusual inflows, human disturbance or long-term climatic changes can weaken stratification and sometimes cause deep-water renewal.', 4);

Naturalists long debated the structure''s function. Its role as a brood chamber was clear, but observations published in 2010 showed that it also assists buoyancy. A female at the sea surface rocks the eggcase to capture air, seals the air with her arms and jets downward. Increasing water pressure compresses the trapped gas. At a suitable depth, the upward force from that gas balances the animal''s weight, allowing neutral buoyancy. The female can then move without continually spending energy merely to avoid sinking.

This system differs from the chambered shell of a true nautilus. An argonaut''s eggcase has no sealed internal chambers, and its air volume changes directly with surrounding pressure. Neutral balance therefore applies over a limited depth range: descend too far and compression reduces lift; rise and expanding gas increases it. Experiments in shallow tanks can also mislead because an argonaut carrying a large gulp of air may be pulled back to the surface before reaching its balance depth. The case is thus neither decorative nor simply protective. It combines reproduction and hydrostatic control, using behaviour to make a fragile structure perform two jobs.', 5);

Numbers alone did not identify what was being counted. Colour, fibre, the direction in which a cord was spun or attached, and the branching arrangement could classify information. Trained officials called khipukamayuq made and interpreted records used for administration, including quantities of people, tribute or stored goods. Some surviving khipus contain numerical relationships such as totals that equal groups of subsidiary entries, strengthening the interpretation of many examples as accounts rather than arbitrary bundles of string.

The disputed question is how far their information extended beyond quantities. Spanish colonial accounts describe khipus being used in connection with histories, messages and other non-numerical knowledge. Researchers have also identified patterned features that might carry categorical or narrative meaning. Yet no bilingual equivalent of the Rosetta Stone securely maps a khipu to a complete spoken text, and much knowledge of reading them was lost after the Spanish conquest. It is therefore too narrow to call every khipu merely a calculator, but too confident to claim that scholars can already read them as ordinary sentences. Their numerical grammar is substantially understood; their wider capacities remain an active field of investigation.', 6);

This sequence helps explain why the two sides of a range can experience sharply different weather, with cloud and precipitation windward but clearer, warmer conditions leeward. It also explains why foehn winds can accelerate snowmelt and increase wildfire danger. However, meteorologists caution that condensation and rainfall are not the only route to a foehn. Warm, dry lee winds can occur even without windward precipitation.

Several mechanisms can contribute. A mountain may block low-level air while allowing potentially warmer, drier air from higher altitude to cross the ridge and descend. Turbulence can mix warmer air downward. Once cloud clears in the lee, additional sunlight can warm the ground and nearby air. The relative importance of these processes varies with the shape of the mountains and with wind speed, temperature, humidity and atmospheric stability. Thus, calling every foehn ''air heated because rain fell'' is too simple. Descent and compression remain central, but the air arriving on the lee side need not be the same parcel that began at the windward valley floor.', 7);

The puzzle is one of collective behaviour. Random collisions should make a hiss containing many frequencies, yet a dune can emit a strong, narrow tone with harmonics. Experiments indicate that grains in the flowing layer partly synchronise their motion. Grain size and the rate at which grains shear past one another are linked to the pitch, helping explain why sorted sand can produce a characteristic note. Moisture or contamination can disrupt the necessary contacts, which is why dry, clean sand is favourable.

Researchers still debate how the dune amplifies and selects its sound. One account treats the flowing layer as a self-synchronising instrument: feedback between grain motion and elastic waves locks many collisions together. Another emphasises a waveguide beneath the surface, where layers with different mechanical properties reinforce particular frequencies. Laboratory avalanches on shallow beds have produced singing without an entire dune, challenging the claim that a deep resonant body is always necessary. This does not make the internal structure irrelevant to loud natural booming; it shows that sound generation and large-scale amplification may be separate problems. The phenomenon is therefore understood better than a century ago, but no single simple analogy explains every observation.', 8);

The closed fore edge may then be gilded or marbled. This decoration does more than embellish the book; it helps conceal traces of the hidden image. To reveal the scene, a reader bends or fans the text block to recreate approximately the angle at which it was painted. A double fore-edge painting contains two images: one appears when the leaves fan in one direction, the other when they fan in the opposite direction.

Dating these works can be difficult. The printed date establishes when the pages were produced, not when their edges were painted. An artist or owner could add a scene years later, and many examples are unsigned. Consequently, a nineteenth-century book bearing a fore-edge scene is not automatically evidence that both book and painting were created together. The object''s surprise depends on concealment; its historical interpretation depends on keeping production of the text, binding and image analytically separate.', 9);

The opening performs a selection rather than a reversal. Without it, rays arriving from many points overlap on the wall and produce undifferentiated illumination. A small aperture admits a narrow bundle from each point, allowing the bundles to form an image. Making the hole smaller can reduce geometric blur, but it also admits less light. The result is a trade-off between geometric sharpness and brightness.

Knowledge of pinhole images long predates photography. Ancient Chinese observers described the phenomenon, and the eleventh-century scholar Ibn al-Haytham analysed image formation in a dark space. Later camera obscuras added lenses to produce brighter, sharper images and mirrors to change their orientation. Portable versions became aids for artists, who could trace a projected scene. Yet the instrument did not itself preserve what it showed. Photography required a further development: a light-sensitive surface and a process that fixed the temporary projection. The camera obscura supplied the optical image, not the lasting record.', 10);
